It seems this is a JavaScriptCode bug, if the function is async, JSC cannot produce the full stack trace.

For example:

```js
async function foo() {
    await Promise.resolve(null);
    throw new Error("something went wrong");
}

async function bar() {
    await foo();
}

(async () => {
    try {
        await bar();
    } catch (err) {
        console.log(err);
    }
})();
```

In Bun, it logs like this:

```
1 | async function foo() {
2 |     await Promise.resolve(null);
3 |     throw new Error("something went wrong");
              ^
error: something went wrong
      at /Users/<user>/Workspace/try-out/log.js:3:10
```

And in Safari (Console Snippets), it logs like this:

```
Error: something went wrong
(anonymous function)
```

The stack trace of the async functions are all lost.

I found a small workaround for getting 1 more stack layer. Just get the stack **before** the `await` happens to see what called the function.

```ts
// this function is used in many places
async function mySharedFunction() {
  console.log("start", new Error().stack);
  await Promise.resolve();
  console.log("end", new Error().stack); // index.js:5
}

async function myRoutine() {
  await Promise.resolve();
  await mySharedFunction();
}

async function main() {
  await Promise.resolve();
  await myRoutine();
}

main();
```

Bun output
```
start Error
    at mySharedFunction (/Users/<user>/index.js:3:28)
    at mySharedFunction (/Users/<user>/index.js:2:32)
    at myRoutine (/Users/<user>/index.js:10:9)
end Error
    at mySharedFunction (/Users/<user>/index.js:5:26)
```


Runtime | Engine | Do traces work after `await`? | Does the workaround work?
-- | -- | -- | --
Bun | JavaScriptCore | ❌ No | ✅ Yes
Safari | JavaScriptCore | ❌ No | ✅ Yes
Firefox | SpiderMonkey | ❌ No | ✅ Yes
Chrome | V8 | ✅ Yes | Not needed
NodeJS | V8 | ✅ Yes | Not needed
Deno | V8 | ✅ Yes | Not needed

For context, this is the V8 output in NodeJS
```
start Error
    at mySharedFunction (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:3:24)
    at myRoutine (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:10:9)
    at async main (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:15:3)
end Error
    at mySharedFunction (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:5:22)
    at async myRoutine (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:10:3)
    at async main (file:///Users/<user>/index.js:15:3)
```

While not ideal, storing the stack in the AsyncLocalStorage works. I made a prototype to demonstrate how this can be done automatically using a `Bun.build` plugin.

Add these files to a new `bun init` root dir. We won't need `index.ts`
<details>

<summary> build-stack.ts </summary>

```ts
import { functionStackPlugin } from './function-stack-plugin-simple';

// Build the stack example with the AsyncLocalStorage plugin
const result = await Bun.build({
  entrypoints: ['./stack-example.ts'],
  outdir: './dist',
  plugins: [functionStackPlugin],
  target: 'bun',
  format: 'esm',
});

if (result.success) {
  console.log('✅ Stack example build successful!');
  console.log('Generated files:');
  for (const output of result.outputs) {
    console.log(`  - ${output.path}`);
  }
} else {
  console.error('❌ Stack example build failed!');
  console.error(result.logs);
} 
```
</details>

<details>

<summary> function-stack-plugin-simple.ts </summary>

```ts
import type { BunPlugin } from "bun";

// Plugin to add AsyncLocalStorage-based function stack tracking
export const functionStackPlugin: BunPlugin = {
  name: "function-stack-tracker",
  setup(build) {
    // Transform JavaScript/TypeScript files
    build.onLoad({ filter: /\.(js|ts|jsx|tsx)$/ }, async (args) => {
      const source = await Bun.file(args.path).text();

      // Add AsyncLocalStorage setup and helper functions
      const transformed = addFunctionStackSetup(source);

      return {
        contents: transformed,
        loader: args.path.endsWith('.tsx') || args.path.endsWith('.jsx') ? 'tsx' : 
                args.path.endsWith('.ts') || args.path.endsWith('.js') ? 'ts' : 'js'
      };
    });
  }
};

function addFunctionStackSetup(source: string): string {
  // Add AsyncLocalStorage import and setup at the top
  const asyncLocalStorageSetup = `
// AsyncLocalStorage for function stack tracking
import { AsyncLocalStorage } from 'node:async_hooks';

const functionStackStorage = new AsyncLocalStorage();

// Helper function to get current stack
export function getFunctionStack() {
  return [...(functionStackStorage.getStore() || [])];
}

// Helper function to print current stack
export function printFunctionStack() {
  const stack = getFunctionStack();
  console.log('Function call stack:');
  stack.forEach((func, index) => {
    console.log(\`  \${index + 1}. \${func}\`);
  });
}

// Helper function to clear stack
export function clearFunctionStack() {
  functionStackStorage.enterWith([]);
}

// Helper function to get stack as string
export function getFunctionStackString() {
  const stack = getFunctionStack();
  return stack.join(' -> ');
}

// Helper function to wrap a function with stack tracking
export function withStackTracking(funcName, fn) {
  return async (...args) => {
    const currentStack = functionStackStorage.getStore() || [];
    const newStack = [...currentStack, funcName];
    return functionStackStorage.run(newStack, () => fn(...args));
  };
}

// Helper function to track function entry
export function trackFunctionEntry(funcName) {
  const currentStack = functionStackStorage.getStore() || [];
  const newStack = [...currentStack, funcName];
  functionStackStorage.enterWith(newStack);
}

// Helper function to track function exit
export function trackFunctionExit() {
  const currentStack = functionStackStorage.getStore() || [];
  if (currentStack.length > 0) {
    const newStack = currentStack.slice(0, -1);
    functionStackStorage.enterWith(newStack);
  }
}

`;

  return asyncLocalStorageSetup + source;
} 
```
</details>

<details>

<summary> stack-example.ts </summary>

```ts
// Example showing how to use AsyncLocalStorage stack tracking

// The plugin will automatically add the helper functions
// We can use them to track function calls manually

async function mySharedFunction() {
  // Track function entry
  trackFunctionEntry('mySharedFunction');

  console.log("start", new Error().stack);
  await Promise.resolve();
  console.log("end", new Error().stack);

  // Print current stack
  printFunctionStack();

  // Track function exit
  trackFunctionExit();
}

async function myRoutine() {
  // Track function entry
  trackFunctionEntry('myRoutine');

  await Promise.resolve();
  await mySharedFunction();

  // Print current stack
  printFunctionStack();

  // Track function exit
  trackFunctionExit();
}

async function main() {
  // Track function entry
  trackFunctionEntry('main');

  await Promise.resolve();
  await myRoutine();

  // Print final stack
  console.log('Final stack:', getFunctionStackString());

  // Track function exit
  trackFunctionExit();
}

// Example with wrapped functions
const wrappedFunction = withStackTracking('wrappedFunction', async () => {
  console.log("Inside wrapped function");
  printFunctionStack();
  await Promise.resolve();
});

async function runExamples() {
  console.log('=== Manual tracking example ===');
  await main();

  console.log('\n=== Wrapped function example ===');
  await wrappedFunction();

  console.log('\n=== Current stack after all examples ===');
  printFunctionStack();
}

runExamples(); 
```
</details>

Once the files are in the root dir, run the following commands

```sh
bun run build-stack.ts
bun run dist/stack-example.js 
```

The output displays that it tracked the functions like this, after awaiting it didn't lose track of the stack.
```
